WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.317
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:18.398
Unit thirty-nine: Global Economy and Development. In this unit, you will critically evaluate development strategies.

4
00:00:19.383 --> 00:00:21.824
development economics.

5
00:00:22.524 --> 00:00:27.949
development economics means the study of how poorer countries can grow and reduce poverty.

6
00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:32.733
Development economics asks why some nations grow faster than others.

7
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.991
Your turn. Say: development economics.

8
00:00:39.691 --> 00:00:41.377
development economics.

9
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:43.942
structural adjustment.

10
00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:52.121
structural adjustment means conditions requiring loan-receiving countries to cut spending and open markets.

11
00:00:52.421 --> 00:00:57.509
Structural adjustment programmes were widely criticised in the 1990s.

12
00:00:58.209 --> 00:01:01.709
Your turn. Say: structural adjustment.

13
00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:05.805
structural adjustment.

14
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:08.607
Washington Consensus.

15
00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:17.948
Washington Consensus means a 1989 set of free-market policy recommendations for developing countries.

16
00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:22.918
The Washington Consensus promoted privatisation and trade liberalisation.

17
00:01:23.618 --> 00:01:27.268
Your turn. Say: Washington Consensus.

18
00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.620
Washington Consensus.

19
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:34.047
middle-income trap.

20
00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:39.115
middle-income trap means when a country's growth stalls before it becomes rich.

21
00:01:39.415 --> 00:01:44.294
Avoiding the middle-income trap requires better skills and innovation.

22
00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:48.262
Your turn. Say: middle-income trap.

23
00:01:50.962 --> 00:01:52.276
middle-income trap.

24
00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:55.039
industrial policy.

25
00:01:55.739 --> 00:02:01.024
industrial policy means government strategy to support particular industries.

26
00:02:01.324 --> 00:02:06.191
South Korea's industrial policy built world-class shipyards.

27
00:02:06.891 --> 00:02:10.484
Your turn. Say: industrial policy.

28
00:02:13.184 --> 00:02:14.917
industrial policy.

29
00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:17.730
foreign direct investment.

30
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:24.331
foreign direct investment means money invested by foreign firms in factories and businesses.

31
00:02:24.631 --> 00:02:28.662
Foreign direct investment has expanded Cambodia's garment sector.

32
00:02:29.362 --> 00:02:32.816
Your turn. Say: foreign direct investment.

33
00:02:35.516 --> 00:02:37.271
foreign direct investment.

34
00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:39.749
aid dependency.

35
00:02:40.449 --> 00:02:45.188
aid dependency means reliance on foreign aid to fund basic government services.

36
00:02:45.488 --> 00:02:50.054
Critics argue that aid dependency weakens local accountability.

37
00:02:50.754 --> 00:02:54.103
Your turn. Say: aid dependency.

38
00:02:56.803 --> 00:02:58.152
aid dependency.

39
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.057
South-South cooperation.

40
00:03:01.757 --> 00:03:06.508
South-South cooperation means partnership between developing countries.

41
00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:11.071
South-South cooperation lets countries share solutions that suit them.

42
00:03:11.771 --> 00:03:15.724
Your turn. Say: South-South cooperation.

43
00:03:18.424 --> 00:03:20.377
South-South cooperation.

44
00:03:21.383 --> 00:03:23.929
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:24.229 --> 00:03:28.202
Ms. Sarah, why did the Washington Consensus fall out of favour?

46
00:03:29.517 --> 00:03:35.095
Its record is, at best, mixed. Structural adjustment cut health budgets in many poor countries.

47
00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:38.963
But didn't free markets help Asia grow?

48
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:47.581
Arguably, East Asia succeeded by ignoring much of that advice. Its industrial policy was highly active.

49
00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:51.707
So is foreign direct investment good or bad?

50
00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:59.858
It is a double-edged sword: jobs, yes, but sometimes low wages and little technology transfer.

51
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.229
And what about Cambodia avoiding the middle-income trap?

52
00:04:05.517 --> 00:04:09.710
The evidence strongly suggests that skills and innovation matter most.

53
00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:14.885
Grammar focus: Critical evaluation language (extended).

54
00:04:15.185 --> 00:04:18.172
Critical evaluation weighs evidence carefully.

55
00:04:18.472 --> 00:04:25.408
The record of structural adjustment is, at best, mixed. Foreign investment is a double-edged sword.

56
00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:29.716
The evidence strongly suggests that skills and innovation matter most.

57
00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:36.730
Evaluate, don't just describe: weigh strengths and weaknesses, then state how strong the evidence is.

58
00:04:37.717 --> 00:04:40.775
Now read and listen. Paths to Prosperity.

59
00:04:41.075 --> 00:04:46.999
Development economics seeks to explain why some countries escape poverty while others remain trapped.

60
00:04:48.283 --> 00:04:58.654
In 1989, economist John Williamson summarised a set of free-market policies that became known as the Washington Consensus.

61
00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:05.618
Many indebted countries were required to adopt structural adjustment programmes in return for loans.

62
00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:13.943
The results were, at best, mixed: some economies stabilised, but public services often suffered.

63
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:21.580
Meanwhile, East Asian economies combined export growth with an active industrial policy.

64
00:05:22.883 --> 00:05:33.149
Today, foreign direct investment remains important, but critics warn that aid dependency and low-wage manufacturing cannot guarantee long-term growth.

65
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:42.440
To avoid the middle-income trap, the evidence suggests, countries must invest in education, infrastructure and innovation.

66
00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:51.531
South-South cooperation increasingly allows developing nations to learn from one another rather than only from the West.

67
00:05:52.817 --> 00:05:55.388
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:55.688 --> 00:06:01.205
A 1989 set of free-market policy recommendations is the blank.

69
00:06:01.505 --> 00:06:07.968
A, Washington Consensus. B, industrial policy. C, South-South cooperation.

70
00:06:11.983 --> 00:06:15.028
The answer is A: Washington Consensus.

71
00:06:16.517 --> 00:06:19.608
'The results were, at best, mixed' expresses blank.

72
00:06:19.908 --> 00:06:25.697
A, a guarded, critical judgement. B, strong praise. C, no opinion.

73
00:06:29.683 --> 00:06:33.343
The answer is A: a guarded, critical judgement.

74
00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.523
'A double-edged sword' means something has blank.

75
00:06:37.823 --> 00:06:44.065
A, both advantages and disadvantages. B, only risks. C, only benefits.

76
00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.524
The answer is A: both advantages and disadvantages.

77
00:06:53.017 --> 00:06:57.222
When growth stalls before a country becomes rich, it is in the blank.

78
00:06:57.522 --> 00:07:03.172
A, middle-income trap. B, aid dependency. C, structural adjustment.

79
00:07:07.183 --> 00:07:10.495
The answer is A: middle-income trap.

80
00:07:11.983 --> 00:07:17.803
According to the text, what did East Asian economies combine with export growth?

81
00:07:18.103 --> 00:07:25.367
A, an active industrial policy. B, structural adjustment. C, aid dependency.

82
00:07:29.383 --> 00:07:33.716
The answer is A: an active industrial policy.

83
00:07:35.217 --> 00:07:42.025
Now it's your turn. Part one: What is the best strategy for Cambodia to become a high-income country?

84
00:07:47.325 --> 00:07:54.039
Part two: Evaluate two strategies critically, using guarded and evidence-based language.

85
00:08:00.339 --> 00:08:15.126
Relying only on garment factories is, at best, a short-term solution. Foreign investment is a double-edged sword. The evidence strongly suggests that investing in education and technology offers the most sustainable path.

86
00:08:15.426 --> 00:08:17.263
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:24.618
Great work today! You learned eight new words and critical evaluation language (extended).

88
00:08:24.918 --> 00:08:28.859
Today's value is hard work. Work hard and never give up.

89
00:08:29.159 --> 00:08:31.521
See you in Unit forty. Goodbye!

90
00:08:31.821 --> 00:08:34.833
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

